 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 , of the most glorious cause for which men can fight.\uc1 \u8221 ? In his Preface, Worsley surveys the traditional metres available to English translators, reviews their characteristics, and finally declares his }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 preference}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  for the Spenserian stanza, the metre of his earlier translation of the }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Odyssey}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 : \uc1 \u8220 ?I do not maintain that a reader who takes eight or nine lines of Homer, and then turns to a single stanza of my }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 translation}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 , will be struck with the metrical analogy. But what I hope and believe to be true is this: that the perusal of a whole book of my translation will leave echoing in the ear a voice accordant in its main swell to the voice of Homer. If that sea-like rolling effect which is so characteristic of him comes out in my pages, though not immediately by the succession of single lines, yet at last by the harmonious accumulation of stanzas, I have made out for the }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 magnificent}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  measure I have chosen a claim which cannot indeed be tested rapidly, but which, if true, will gradually recommend itself to favour.\uc1 \u8221 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Perhaps the Spenserian stanza works more effectively among people who are thoroughly familiar with it (as Worsley seems to claim) but to any modern reader the problems it presents as a vehicle for translating Homer are obvious enough (and were pointed out by some contemporary reviewers). That \uc1 \u8220 ?sea-like rolling effect which is so characteristic of [Homer]\uc1 \u8221 ? (whatever that means exactly) is, so far as I can tell, quite absent, replaced by an irritatingly predictable stanza form, which often interrupts whatever movement the verse is generating. And Worsely\uc1 \u8217 ?s diction compounds the problem for the modern reader. The artificially aged language\uc1 \u8212 ?especially when it serves to provide the necessary rhyme (e.g., fain/main, fell/quell, and so on)\uc1 \u8212 ?robs the English of any urgency and tends to turn the poem into yet another \uc1 \u8220 ?quainte storie.\uc1 \u8221 ? It\uc1 \u8217 ?s almost as if Worsley, like many of his contemporaries, feels that Homer in English requires an artificial language that is no longer relevant to his century.}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Of this translation Bush makes the following observation: "Vol 2, containing Books xiii-xxiv, was translated by [John] Connington, with the exception of twelve stanzas of Book xiii, which are Worsley\uc1 \u8217 ?s. Vol. 1 is dated 1865."}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af4 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \