33 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \uc1 \u160 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Herschel offers an interesting Preface, in which he mounts a stout defence of the hexameter as the most appropriate metre for translating Homer.\uc1 \u160 ?He also explains his own approach to the task of translating Homer\uc1 \u8217 ?s Greek: \uc1 \u8220 ?Though a careful interpretation of the Greek it does not profess to be minutely close, much less a strictly literal translation: while on the other hand it eschews altogether any attempt to clothe the simple and rude majesty of the great original in such amplitude of decorated wording as to conceal its outlines.\uc1 \u8221 ?\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Of the recurring epithets, he has this to say, \uc1 \u8220 ?But to be systematical reminded of these particulars almost whenever the persons or things so characterized are mentioned, is assuredly more in the nature of a blemish than a beauty.\uc1 \u8221 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Herschel is also one of the very few translators who with the use of italics deliberately calls attention to his additions to the text: \uc1 \u8220 ?good faith both to the original and the reader requires that expletory words or phrases should be distinguished by some typographical difference.\uc1 \u8221 ?\uc1 \u160 ?The translation, as a whole, is not particularly inspiring or memorable and does little to demonstrate the poetic superiority of the hexameter, as a }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 contemporary}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  reviewer remarked: }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \uc1 \u160 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li720 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin720 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 His translation, too, is rendered by hexameters. This we take to be a thorough mistake. . . .The whole question . . . of English }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 hexameters}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025  and qu